React Router v6 introduced significant changes from v5. Here are the patterns I use in every project.

## Layout Routes

Layout routes let you wrap child routes with shared UI:

```jsx
const router = createBrowserRouter([
  {
    path: "/",
    element: <MainLayout />,
    children: [
      { index: true, element: <Home /> },
      { path: "about", element: <About /> },
    ],
  },
]);
```

## Nested Routes with Outlet

The `Outlet` component renders child routes inside a parent:

```jsx
const MainLayout = () => (
  <div>
    <Navbar />
    <Outlet />
    <Footer />
  </div>
);
```

## Programmatic Navigation

Use `useNavigate` for imperative navigation:

```jsx
const navigate = useNavigate();
navigate("/dashboard", { replace: true });
```

## Catch-All Routes

Always include a catch-all for 404 handling:

```jsx
{ path: "*", element: <NotFound /> }

CSS has evolved rapidly. Here are the techniques I use daily that have changed how I write styles.

## Container Queries

Container queries let components respond to their container's size, not just the viewport:

```css
@container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card { flex-direction: row; }
}
```

## The :has() Selector

The parent selector CSS never had:

```css
.form:has(:invalid) {
  border-color: red;
}
```

## CSS Nesting

Native CSS nesting is now supported in all major browsers:

```css
.card {
  padding: 1rem;
  & .title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }
  &:hover {
    background: #f0f0f0;
  }
}
```

## Cascade Layers

Control specificity with `@layer`:

```css
@layer base, components, utilities;
@layer base { body { margin: 0; } }
@layer utilities { .p-4 { padding: 1rem; } }
```

These features reduce JavaScript dependency for styling logic and improve performance.
